  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGES:\n\n- Gusty winds on Saturday creating choppy conditions for area\n  lakes, especially across northwest Montana.\n\n- Cooler and showery by early to mid next week.\n\nHigh pressure conditions continue through tomorrow creating stable\nconditions. What does that produce in terms of weather, valley\ninversions, light winds, limited afternoon warming, and cold\nmornings with patchy freezing fog and low stratus. \n\nBy Saturday afternoon, our valley inversions break and\ntemperatures warm into the lower 60s western MT and upper 60s low\n70s in north-central ID. A dry cold front will approach from the\nwest pushed by strong mid-level winds. As the atmosphere \ndestabilizes those winds will translate down into valleys. Expect \nwinds 15 to 25 mph in valleys with some locations in northwest MT\nseeing gusts to 35 mph, while mountains receive gusts 35 to 45 \nmph. For this reason, we have issued a lake wind advisory for \nthose wanting to enjoy one potentially last taste of summer. Winds\nwill create choppy conditions on area lakes after 2 pm, so plan \naccordingly. \n\nHigh pressure temporarily rebuilds on Sunday with a return to\nstable conditions. Expect light winds, cool mornings with patchy \nfog, and afternoon temperatures that do not quite hit full \npotential.\n\nThe models keep the Northern Rockies 'in between' or what we call\nsplit flow. The polar jet stream remains well north in Canada,\nwhile an area of low pressure is expected to close off over\nCalifornia. Light showers, cool temperatures, and breezy\nafternoons are expected, with no major systems currently. If that\nlow pressure over California ejects more northeasterly than the \ncurrent forecast, a more exciting forecast may result.",
